::ALG2_U04_UA_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_UA_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which sequence is exponential? {
= 2, 6, 18, 54#Correct. The outputs multiply by 3 each step.
~ 2, 5, 8, 11#This is linear.
~ 20, 17, 14, 11#This is linear decrease.
~ 1, 4, 9, 16#This is not a constant ratio.
}

::ALG2_U04_UA_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_UA_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>What factor represents 12% growth? {
~ 0.12#This is only the rate.
= 1.12#Correct. Growth uses 1 + 0.12.
~ 0.88#This is 12% decay.
~ 1.88#This is not 12% growth.
}

::ALG2_U04_UA_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_UA_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A car worth 20000 depreciates 10% annually. Which model fits? {
~ V(t)=20000(1.10)<sup>t</sup>#This is growth.
~ V(t)=0.90(20000)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses parameters.
= V(t)=20000(0.90)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. Losing 10% means 90% remains.
~ V(t)=20000-10t#This is fixed decrease.
}

::ALG2_U04_UA_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_UA_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is log<sub>3</sub>(81)? {
~ 3#3 cubed is 27.
~ 9#3 to the ninth is too large.
~ 27#This is not the exponent.
= 4#Correct. 3 to the fourth power is 81.
}

::ALG2_U04_UA_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_UA_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Expand log<sub>2</sub>(ab). {
= log<sub>2</sub>(a)+log<sub>2</sub>(b)#Correct. Use the product property.
~ log<sub>2</sub>(a)-log<sub>2</sub>(b)#This is quotient.
~ log<sub>2</sub>(a+b)#No sum property.
~ log<sub>2</sub>(a/b)#This is quotient.
}

::ALG2_U04_UA_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_UA_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve log<sub>2</sub>(x)=5. {
~ x=10#This multiplies.
= x=32#Correct. Rewrite as x=2<sup>5</sup>.
~ x=7#This adds.
~ x=3#This subtracts.
}

::ALG2_U04_UA_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_UA_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 600(1.04)<sup>t</sup>=900 for t. {
~ t=log(1.04)/log(900/600)#This reverses the ratio.
~ t=900/600/1.04#This is linear thinking.
= t=log(900/600)/log(1.04)#Correct. Divide by 600, then use logs.
~ t=900-600-1.04#This is not exponential solving.
}

::ALG2_U04_UA_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_UA_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 4 standards synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What is the inverse of y=10<sup>x</sup>? {
~ y=x<sup>10</sup>#This is a power function.
~ y=10x#This is linear.
~ y=x/10#This is linear division.
= y=log<sub>10</sub>(x)#Correct. A base-10 logarithm reverses the exponential.
}

